<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Fully Aware nightly-fix LaunchAgent: the executor lane of the defect register.

  At 02:30 local it runs tools/nightly-fix.sh, which takes the OLDEST small
  defect the register marks nightly-eligible (owner codex, fix_scope repo-pr,
  size S, a real verify, a remote), fixes it in a FRESH clone under
  ~/code/.nightly-fix/ using the Codex CLI, proves it with the item's pr_check,
  and opens ONE pull request. It never touches an existing checkout, never
  pushes to a default branch, never force-pushes, and never merges.
  Merge is Anthony's.

  What the Codex process is allowed to reach, stated exactly. Its shell runs
  under a workspace-write sandbox, which covers model-generated shell commands
  and nothing else. So on top of that, every MCP server named in the global
  Codex config is switched off by name on the command line, the whole plugin
  system is switched off (that is what removes the mail, chat, notes, drive,
  GitHub and computer-history connectors), and the app, browser, computer-use,
  code-mode, memory and chronicle features are switched off. Those are command
  line overrides for the one run; the global config file is never edited. The
  lane VERIFIES this before it launches the model, by running the Codex server
  list with the same overrides and refusing to start unless every row reads
  disabled.

  What is NOT restricted: reads. A workspace-write sandbox limits writes, not
  reads, so Codex can still read files elsewhere on this Mac. The answer to
  that is on the way out: before committing, the lane lists every changed and
  untracked path and refuses to stage an untracked file named like a
  credential, anything over a megabyte, or anything resolving outside the
  clone. The item's own check also runs inside a Codex seatbelt that denies
  the network and denies writes outside the clone.

  The exit code means something now. 0 is a pull request opened or a
  deliberate stand down; 2 is a lock, config or safety refusal; 3 and above
  say the lane tried and could not finish, one number per reason. The morning
  digest reads that number out of launchctl.

  02:30 is deliberate: it finishes long before the 05:45 boot pack and the 06:15
  daily scan, so the morning brief can report what the night did.

  NOT ARMED. This file is inert until tools/install-nightly-fix.sh is run with
  its apply option after the defect-register branch merges. Editing this plist
  changes nothing on its own; the installed copy in
  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/ is what launchd reads.

  Writes land in gitignored state/ only (state/nightly-fix/ for prompts, logs
  and attempts; state/logs/ for the launchd streams) plus the throwaway clone.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.anthonyflores.fully-aware.nightly-fix</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>/Users/anthonyflores/code/fully-aware/tools/nightly-fix.sh</string>
    </array>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>/Users/anthonyflores/code/fully-aware</string>

    <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
    <dict>
        <key>Hour</key>
        <integer>2</integer>
        <key>Minute</key>
        <integer>30</integer>
    </dict>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/anthonyflores/code/fully-aware/state/logs/nightly-fix.out.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/anthonyflores/code/fully-aware/state/logs/nightly-fix.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
